1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,043
A drop of pond water.

2
00:00:02,043 --> 00:00:03,745
A red tide in the sea.

3
00:00:03,745 --> 00:00:06,144
A mosquito bite that brings malaria.

4
00:00:06,544 --> 00:00:09,853
All three come from single
cells with a true nucleus.

5
00:00:10,303 --> 00:00:14,433
Diya put one drop of pond water
under the college microscope.

6
00:00:14,433 --> 00:00:15,935
The drop was alive.

7
00:00:15,935 --> 00:00:18,125
Cells swam, crawled and spun.

8
00:00:18,475 --> 00:00:19,805
These are protists.

9
00:00:20,255 --> 00:00:24,279
By the end, you will know the five
groups of Protista, with an example

10
00:00:24,279 --> 00:00:25,179
of each.

11
00:00:25,604 --> 00:00:28,446
Let's zoom into that
drop, one level at a time.

12
00:00:28,796 --> 00:00:30,122
Here is the pond.

13
00:00:30,122 --> 00:00:32,407
Still water, green at the edges.

14
00:00:32,907 --> 00:00:34,325
Zoom into one drop.

15
00:00:34,325 --> 00:00:36,196
Dozens of cells move about.

16
00:00:36,696 --> 00:00:38,229
Zoom in on one cell.

17
00:00:38,229 --> 00:00:39,446
This is Euglena.

18
00:00:39,446 --> 00:00:43,987
A long flagellum pulls it along,
and there is a short one beside it.

19
00:00:44,487 --> 00:00:49,118
Instead of a cell wall, it has a
flexible, protein rich layer called the

20
00:00:49,118 --> 00:00:50,018
pellicle.

21
00:00:49,868 --> 00:00:52,742
Inside, a nucleus, and green chloroplasts.

22
00:00:53,392 --> 00:00:57,051
Kingdom Protista holds all
single celled eukaryotes.

23
00:00:57,401 --> 00:01:01,731
Each cell has a true nucleus, and
other parts wrapped in membranes.

24
00:01:01,731 --> 00:01:03,945
Some swim with flagella or cilia.

25
00:01:04,395 --> 00:01:05,838
Most live in water.

26
00:01:05,838 --> 00:01:10,417
They reproduce asexually, and also
sexually, when two cells fuse to form

27
00:01:10,417 --> 00:01:11,317
a zygote.

28
00:01:11,889 --> 00:01:13,853
Protista has five main groups.

29
00:01:13,853 --> 00:01:15,876
Let's go through them in order.

30
00:01:16,226 --> 00:01:17,126
One.

31
00:01:16,754 --> 00:01:17,749
Chrysophytes.

32
00:01:17,749 --> 00:01:20,932
Diatoms and golden algae,
also called desmids.

33
00:01:20,932 --> 00:01:24,404
They float as plankton in
fresh water and the sea.

34
00:01:25,154 --> 00:01:30,035
A diatom's wall is two thin shells that
fit together like a soap box, filled

35
00:01:30,035 --> 00:01:31,021
with silica.

36
00:01:31,021 --> 00:01:35,871
Their walls pile up as diatomaceous
earth, used for polishing, and filtering

37
00:01:35,871 --> 00:01:37,121
oils and syrups.

38
00:01:37,871 --> 00:01:38,771
Two.

39
00:01:38,404 --> 00:01:39,564
Dinoflagellates.

40
00:01:39,564 --> 00:01:42,553
Mostly marine, with
stiff cellulose plates.

41
00:01:42,553 --> 00:01:47,392
One flagellum lies along the cell,
the other across it, in a groove.

42
00:01:48,142 --> 00:01:53,236
When red dinoflagellates like Gonyaulax
multiply fast, the sea turns red.

43
00:01:53,236 --> 00:01:57,019
This is a red tide, and
its toxins can kill fish.

44
00:01:57,769 --> 00:01:58,669
Three.

45
00:01:58,381 --> 00:02:01,978
Euglenoids, like Euglena,
found in still fresh water.

46
00:02:02,728 --> 00:02:03,628
Four.

47
00:02:03,281 --> 00:02:04,286
Slime moulds.

48
00:02:04,286 --> 00:02:06,870
They feed on decaying twigs and leaves.

49
00:02:06,870 --> 00:02:11,376
In good conditions, they gather into
a plasmodium, which can spread over

50
00:02:11,376 --> 00:02:12,381
several feet.

51
00:02:13,131 --> 00:02:17,494
In bad conditions, it forms fruiting
bodies with spores at the tips.

52
00:02:17,494 --> 00:02:22,306
The spores have true walls, survive
for years, and travel on air currents.

53
00:02:23,056 --> 00:02:23,956
Five.

54
00:02:23,642 --> 00:02:24,542
Protozoans.

55
00:02:24,542 --> 00:02:27,795
All heterotrophs, either
predators or parasites.

56
00:02:27,795 --> 00:02:31,236
They are thought to be
early relatives of animals.

57
00:02:31,986 --> 00:02:35,208
Amoeba pushes out false
feet called pseudopodia.

58
00:02:35,208 --> 00:02:39,552
Trypanosoma swims with a flagellum,
and causes sleeping sickness.

59
00:02:39,552 --> 00:02:44,023
Paramoecium beats thousands of
cilia to sweep food into its gullet.

60
00:02:44,023 --> 00:02:48,483
Plasmodium has an infectious spore
like stage, and causes malaria.

61
00:02:49,083 --> 00:02:50,480
Euglena has a trick.

62
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:52,899
Let's compare it in light and in dark.

63
00:02:53,249 --> 00:02:56,869
In sunlight, its chloroplasts
make food, like a plant.

64
00:02:56,869 --> 00:02:58,272
That is autotrophic.

65
00:02:58,722 --> 00:03:02,587
Without sunlight, it hunts
smaller organisms, like an animal.

66
00:03:02,587 --> 00:03:04,051
That is heterotrophic.

67
00:03:04,651 --> 00:03:05,551
Your turn.

68
00:03:05,531 --> 00:03:08,716
Euglena is kept in a
dark cupboard for a week.

69
00:03:08,716 --> 00:03:09,910
Will it starve?

70
00:03:09,910 --> 00:03:11,204
Pause and decide.

71
00:03:14,404 --> 00:03:15,304
No!

72
00:03:14,868 --> 00:03:18,437
In the dark, it switches to
eating smaller organisms.

73
00:03:18,437 --> 00:03:20,514
One cell, two ways to feed.

74
00:03:21,164 --> 00:03:22,227
Remember this.

75
00:03:22,227 --> 00:03:25,289
Protista means one cell,
with a true nucleus.

76
00:03:25,889 --> 00:03:27,597
Now, a worked example.

77
00:03:27,597 --> 00:03:28,497
Six clues.

78
00:03:28,477 --> 00:03:30,246
Name the group for each.

79
00:03:30,596 --> 00:03:32,770
Silica walls, like a soap box.

80
00:03:32,770 --> 00:03:34,570
Chrysophytes, the diatoms.

81
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:36,065
Red tides.

82
00:03:36,065 --> 00:03:38,625
Dinoflagellates, like Gonyaulax.

83
00:03:39,075 --> 00:03:41,281
A pellicle instead of a wall.

84
00:03:41,281 --> 00:03:42,203
Euglenoids.

85
00:03:42,653 --> 00:03:45,490
A plasmodium that forms fruiting bodies.

86
00:03:45,490 --> 00:03:46,598
Slime moulds.

87
00:03:47,098 --> 00:03:48,168
False feet.

88
00:03:48,168 --> 00:03:49,761
Amoeboid protozoans.

89
00:03:49,761 --> 00:03:52,843
And malaria, sporozoans like Plasmodium.

90
00:03:53,493 --> 00:03:54,959
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:03:55,309 --> 00:03:59,423
Many students think slime moulds are
fungi, because of the word mould.

92
00:03:59,823 --> 00:04:02,703
But NCERT places slime moulds in Protista.

93
00:04:02,703 --> 00:04:04,773
They are saprophytic protists.

94
00:04:05,423 --> 00:04:06,888
Where is this asked?

95
00:04:06,888 --> 00:04:11,493
As statements on the protist groups,
or matching each organism to its group

96
00:04:11,493 --> 00:04:12,398
or disease.

97
00:04:12,998 --> 00:04:14,216
Exam level now.

98
00:04:14,216 --> 00:04:16,012
Read the four statements.

99
00:04:16,012 --> 00:04:17,705
Which ones are correct?

100
00:04:17,705 --> 00:04:18,974
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:24,374 --> 00:04:25,645
The answer is C.

102
00:04:25,645 --> 00:04:27,359
Statements one and three.

103
00:04:27,859 --> 00:04:28,948
Two is wrong.

104
00:04:28,948 --> 00:04:32,256
Euglenoids have a pellicle,
not a cellulose wall.

105
00:04:32,756 --> 00:04:34,085
Four is wrong.

106
00:04:34,085 --> 00:04:38,602
Trypanosoma is a flagellated
protozoan, not a ciliated one.

107
00:04:39,202 --> 00:04:40,658
Here is your recap.

108
00:04:40,658 --> 00:04:42,155
Screenshot this card.

109
00:04:45,355 --> 00:04:48,353
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

110
00:04:48,753 --> 00:04:49,653
One.

111
00:04:49,292 --> 00:04:50,814
What is a pellicle?

112
00:04:51,714 --> 00:04:52,614
Two.

113
00:04:52,278 --> 00:04:54,159
What causes a red tide?

114
00:04:55,059 --> 00:04:55,959
Three.

115
00:04:55,718 --> 00:04:59,830
Name the four kinds of
protozoans, with one example each.

116
00:05:00,930 --> 00:05:03,998
Next time: green and
black spots on old bread.

117
00:05:03,998 --> 00:05:06,306
Plant, animal, or something else?

118
00:05:06,306 --> 00:05:08,287
We find out in kingdom Fungi.
